# EasyNet / Axon Commit Plan 2

## Purpose

This plan supersedes `commit-plan` as the next execution plan after the daemon
Invocation migration. The previous plan mostly closed the runtime boundary:
EasyNet-Cli now exposes daemon Invocation as the product call path, EasyNet
backend calls the daemon over gRPC, and Axon remains the protocol owner.

This version also folds in the 2026-06-02 product/user-case review:
`2026-06-02-015131-user-case.txt`. That review adds a product-surface
constraint that the architecture ledger alone misses: EasyNet must let a user
discover, trust, authorize, transact with, and audit stranger agents without
knowing the internal Axon/EAL/MCP machinery.

The remaining work should not be treated as one flat backlog. It has three
different axes:

1. **Naming / ontology cleanup** — cheap in implementation but highly coupled
   across wire names, prompts, catalog rows, receipt display, and backend
   routing.
2. **Audit / capability completeness** — harder, less cosmetic, and the part
   that gates third-party verifiability of EasyNet's architectural claims.
3. **Product command-surface convergence** — the CLI/browser vocabulary that
   turns Invocation, receipts, discovery, trust, permission, and adapters into
   a usable marketplace workflow.

The P0 naming work must share one coordinated wire-break window. It must not
crowd out receipt-chain closure, because receipt closure is the proof surface
that lets outsiders audit the system.

## Core Verdict

| Area | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Daemon Invocation migration | Mostly closed. Keep guards; do not reopen JSON-control product calls. |
| `<self>` alias + flat namespaces | One coordinated wire-breaking migration. Treat as a single release train, not independent fixes. |
| Ability / Skill ontology | Must be normalized before further public docs or SDK prose. This is not just wording; it drives what is allowed to cross Axon. |
| Receipt verifier closure | Underpriced in the old table. Raise to P0.5 because it gates auditable receipt-chain claims. |
| FFI bidi terminal E2E | Closed in EasyNet-Cli. Keep the daemon happy-path smoke as a regression guard. |
| Federation discovery unification | Important, but after the namespace release unless it is bundled as part of the same name-contract cleanup. |
| Product command surface | Not converged. `discover`, `trust`, `permission/policy`, `whoami`, ability import front doors, and family projection are the missing user-facing closure. |
| Ability adapters | Runtime model already supports Shell/HTTP/EAL/MCP ability execution, but the CLI front doors are under-marketed. Add ergonomic `ability from-*` commands instead of changing the execution model. |
| Ability family/tree | Useful for discovery, policy scope, and rendering. Do not make it part of URA addressing or Invocation semantics. |
| Project structure | Not healthy enough for the next migration wave. `src/services/invocation_transport/daemon_invocation_service.rs` is 11,399 lines and mixes tonic RPC facade, dispatch routing, session/bidi plumbing, target resolution, receipt/ledger helpers, quota, peer signing, and tests. |
| Economy / pricing / settlement | Real future value, but do not implement before receipt closure. Keep as Axon RFC stub + no local fork. |

## Invariants

1. Axon owns protocol shape, canonical bytes, signatures, admission,
   receipts, stream/bidi semantics, verifier behavior, and cross-language
   SDK contracts.
2. EasyNet-Cli daemon owns product/device/Hub policy: local identity,
   keyring forwarding, plugins, EAL/Mission, local resources, sessions,
   device abilities, and Hub/device behavior.
3. EasyNet backend owns product surface: HTTP/API, browser UX, DB
   projections, auth/session UX, dashboards, and product aggregation.
4. Public callable surfaces collapse to Ability. Skill, plugin, tool,
   handler, raw RPC, HTTP endpoint, and gRPC method are implementation or
   transport details unless explicitly exposed as an Ability.
5. Every product invocation crossing a process or language boundary must
   preserve the seven-tuple:
   `caller, callee, ability, subject, nonce, causal_context, args -> receipt`.
6. JSON control remains boot/status/diagnostics only. Product calls do not
   return to `control.sock`.
7. Ability family is projection metadata only. URA routing and Invocation stay
   flat.
8. Trust and permission are separate product questions: trust answers how much
   confidence a caller/callee has; permission answers what an authorized caller
   may do.
9. Structure refactors must preserve protocol behavior first. Moving code out
   of a large service file is allowed; changing Invocation semantics, receipt
   fields, admission order, or stream terminal behavior is not part of that
   refactor.

## Execution Axes

### Axis A — Naming And Ontology Release

This axis is coupled and should land under one release carrier, tentatively:

`AXON-RFC-001 v4.1.6 / EasyNet namespace cleanup release`

#### Namespace Target Model

The namespace target should behave like DNS, but with receipts and policy:
human-friendly names resolve into canonical URAs, URAs resolve into live
placement and ability descriptors, and every resolution is cacheable,
verifiable, and policy-filtered. Do not treat dotted ability names as the
whole namespace system.

| Layer | Owner | Current State | Target Shape | Consumers |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| L0 Realm | Axon | Realm is already encoded in URA as `easynet:///r/<realm>/...`; RFC says realm is a DNS name. | Realm is the root zone. It selects the hub/directory authority and verification root. | SDKs, daemon, backend, browser URA parser. |
| L1 Role path | Axon | URA roles exist: user/device/agent/ability/hub/resource. | Strict role grammar is canonical identity. No product shortcut may become a routable address. | Receipt verifier, admission, directory, SDKs. |
| L2 Friendly front door | EasyNet-Cli + EasyNet | CLI still uses AgentId and dotted ability names as ergonomic input. | Friendly names are aliases only: `claude.chat`, `device.fs.read`, `discover "intent"` resolve to URA + ability descriptor before Invocation. | CLI, browser UX, LLM prompts. |
| L3 Directory record | Axon contract; daemon/backend project it | Backend catalog is already keyed by `(OwnerAgentURA, Descriptor.Name)`, but discovery verbs are fragmented. | Directory maps canonical URA to descriptors, endpoints, host placement, public keys, trust metadata, policy hints, and TTL. | Hub, daemon cache, backend aggregator, SDK discovery. |
| L4 Ability descriptor | Axon contract; CLI supplies implementation metadata | Descriptor fields partly live in CLI manifests and backend projections. | Ability descriptor is cross-language contract; execution mode stays CLI-local. | Discovery ranking, policy, docs, SDK call builders. |
| L5 Projection namespace | EasyNet-Cli + EasyNet | Family/tree and categories are not first-class enough. | `family`, `parent`, tags, categories, and marketplace facets are projections, not addresses. | `discover --tree`, policy scopes, UI grouping. |
| L6 Resolution cache | Daemon + backend | There are local registries and backend catalogs, but no explicit DNS-like cache contract. | Cache entries carry TTL, issuer, generation/revision, stale-while-revalidate behavior, and receipt/audit pointers. | CLI fast path, backend pages, SDK clients. |

The DNS-like lookup chain should be:

```text
user input / prompt term
  -> local alias table
  -> local daemon registry
  -> realm directory cache
  -> hub/federation discover
  -> canonical Ability URA + descriptor + placement
  -> complete Axon Invocation
  -> receipt
```

This gives efficient use without making the user type URAs, while preserving
the invariant that only URA is routable protocol identity.

