### Axis D — Structural Health Release

This axis removes the main EasyNet-Cli maintainability bottleneck before the
next protocol/name/discovery wave piles more behavior onto it.

#### Ideal EasyNet-Cli Repository Shape

EasyNet-Cli should read as a daemon product runtime, not as a second protocol
repo and not as the EasyNet backend. The target structure is:

```text
src/
  bin/                  # thin process entrypoints only
  cli/                  # clap command tree + presentation; no daemon internals
  daemon/               # process lifecycle, sockets, boot, config wiring
  daemon_api/           # stable Rust client surface for controlling daemon
  ffi/                  # C ABI facade over daemon_api and Invocation client
  invocation/           # daemon Axon Invocation transport adapters
    service.rs          # thin tonic facade
    deps.rs             # dependency bundle / builder
    unary.rs            # Invoke routing
    stream.rs           # InvokeStream routing
    bidi.rs             # InvokeBidi routing
    target_resolver.rs
    local_bidi.rs
    session_accept.rs
    remote_bidi.rs
    ledger_projection.rs
    quota.rs
  abilities/            # EasyNet ability manifests/descriptors and import UX
  execution/            # Shell/HTTP/MCP/EAL execution adapters
  eal/                  # Mission/EAL compiler/interpreter only
  agents/               # local hosted-agent lifecycle and profiles
  plugins/              # plugin host, builtin plugins, sidecar/install logic
  identity/             # keyring, self identity, trust anchor cells
  directory/            # local/federated directory cache and resolver
  policy/               # daemon-owned policy UX/storage wrappers
  persistence/          # file/db config stores; no transport semantics
  support/              # small platform utilities only
```

The point is not to rename everything immediately. The point is to make each
folder name answer one question:

| Folder | Owns | Must Not Own |
|---|---|---|
| `cli/` | User command grammar, help text, terminal presentation. | Protocol semantics, daemon boot, ability execution. |
| `daemon/` | Process lifecycle, sockets, boot wiring, config reload. | Axon Invocation canonicalization or backend product state. |
| `daemon_api/` | Stable Rust API for starting/stopping/status/invocation endpoint. | One-method-per-ability APIs. |
| `ffi/` | ABI-safe wrapper over daemon_api and generic Invocation. | Local business logic or Axon verifier semantics. |
| `invocation/` | Daemon-side binding to Axon Invocation RPC and local dispatch adapters. | Axon protocol definitions, receipt verification, product UI. |
| `abilities/` | EasyNet-local ability authoring/import metadata and descriptor projection. | Cross-language canonical descriptor contract once Axon owns it. |
| `execution/` | Shell/REST/MCP/EAL execution backends. | Network-visible ability identity. |
| `agents/` | Hosted local agent lifecycle, profiles, local agent registry. | Device/Hub protocol routing. |
| `plugins/` | Plugin loading, builtin plugin handlers, sidecars. | Core Invocation dispatch or policy truth. |
| `identity/` | Keyring, trust anchors, self identity, signing material access. | Admission semantics beyond calling Axon-owned checks. |
| `directory/` | DNS-like resolver cache, federated directory projection. | Canonical URA grammar. |
| `policy/` | Product policy storage/UX wrappers and consent profile integration. | Axon policy/admission contract shape. |
| `persistence/` | TOML/JSON/redb stores and path conventions. | Transport routing decisions. |
| `support/` | OS/network/process/time helpers. | Domain objects. |

Current names can migrate gradually:

