## Priority Repricing

| Old Priority | New Priority | Item | Reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| P1 | P0.5 | Receipt verifier closure | This is the third-party audit proof surface. It is more important than FFI bidi unless a binding release is imminent. |
| P0 | P0 grouped | `<self>` alias, flat namespace, ontology cleanup | These are one wire-break release. Treating them separately creates repeated compatibility windows. |
| P1 | DONE | FFI bidi terminal E2E | Completed as a regression-guarded binding path; no longer competes with receipt closure priority. |
| P1 | P1/P2 | Federation discovery unification | Important for SDK cleanliness; can wait unless bundled with namespace cleanup. |
| Missing | P0 | Discover/trust/permission CLI surface | This is the product route to stranger-agent usage; without it the system remains an engineer-facing runtime. |
| Missing | P0.5 | Ability adapter front doors | The execution model exists, but users need visible verbs for CLI/API/MCP-to-ability packaging. |
| Missing | P0 | Invocation service split | A 11k-line service is a change-amplifier. Namespace/discovery/receipt work will keep colliding unless the dispatch surface is split first or in parallel. |
| P2 | P2 | Economy / settlement | Valuable, but dangerous to implement before receipt closure and Axon-owned shape. |

## Release Gates

### Gate A — Namespace Release

- No normative docs define Ability as a Skill component.
- No new active `<self>.*` ability names.
- No new flat daemon system ability namespace outside `device.*` / `hub.*`.
- Friendly names, aliases, and family projection do not appear as canonical
  routable identities in receipts.
- Directory/discover records expose TTL, issuer/source, descriptor version, and
  policy-filtered visibility.
- Backend routes canonical names and legacy aliases during compatibility window.
- Hub/device join, session, register-device-pubkey, keyring, and discover flows pass
  with canonical names.
- Historical receipt display can render legacy names without rewriting records.
- Lint rejects active `<self>.*` or flat system registrations after cleanup commit.

### Gate B — Receipt Closure

- `easynet verify` validates at least one multi-receipt lifecycle chain.
- `easynet verify` fails on tampered parent hash.
- `easynet verify` fails on missing causal parent unless explicitly configured for
  partial/offline mode.
- CLI daemon exposes read-only receipt lookup without owning Axon verification logic.
- Backend exposes receipt links as product projections only.
- Hosted-agent signer/attestation is not claimed until schema and fixtures exist.

### Gate C — No Regression To Old Runtime Boundary

- `scripts/check-daemon-invocation-migration.sh` stays green.
- No production caller constructs JSON control `Invoke`, `Subscribe`, or `OpenBidi`
  product frames.
- No Axon SDK API starts `easynet-daemon`.
- No backend code hosts device abilities directly.
- No CLI code defines canonical Invocation bytes or receipt verification semantics.

### Gate D — User-Case Command Surface

- `easynet whoami` shows the current identity context without exposing Axon
  internals as the primary UX.
- `easynet discover "<intent>"` can return at least local and federated/catalog
  ability candidates through one user-facing path.
- `trust` commands do not imply authorization, and `permission/policy` commands
  do not imply trust.
- Owner can grant/revoke/list permission for an ability or ability family
  projection.
- `ability from-cli`, `ability from-restful-api`, `ability from-openapi`, and
  `ability from-mcp` package existing execution variants instead of creating a
  second ability model.
- `discover --tree` renders ability family projection without changing URA or
  Invocation semantics.
- Wallet/agreement/payment remains reserved until Axon owns the settlement
  contract.

### Gate E — Structure Health

- `daemon_invocation_service.rs` is a thin tonic facade, not a god object.
- No extracted module owns Axon protocol semantics that belong in Axon.
- No refactor commit changes admission order, quota order, receipt fields,
  terminal stream behavior, or LocalRuntime fallback semantics.
- Target resolution, bidi/session adapters, ledger projection, and quota
  metering each have focused tests.
- Inline service tests are moved or reduced enough that future protocol changes
  touch the smallest relevant module.

## Do Not Do

1. Do not split `<self>` removal and flat namespace cleanup into separate release
   windows unless a release manager explicitly accepts two compatibility cycles.
2. Do not add economy fields to CLI manifests as authoritative settlement terms.
   CLI qualitative `[cost]` metadata is not billing.
3. Do not promote `AgentId` into Axon as a routable address. Keep AgentId as
   logical/front-door identity and URA as routable protocol address.
4. Do not mark receipt-chain audit claims as complete while verifier only checks
   one receipt.
5. Do not delete legacy aliases before one release window with telemetry or tests
   proving no supported caller depends on them.
6. Do not let backend DB projections become canonical state or receipt truth.
7. Do not put ability family/tree into URA routing or Invocation addressing.
8. Do not hide shipped Shell/HTTP/MCP adapter capability behind internal
   constructors only; expose product verbs if the user-case depends on them.
9. Do not ship wallet/agreement/payment as local CLI truth before Axon defines
   the contract.
10. Do not hide semantic changes inside the structural split. If a behavior
    change is needed, put it in a separate commit after the extraction tests
    prove the old behavior.

